From 85d5b79a537860b5c2eaf8148196c633e712855a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adam Durham <amdnative@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 18:39:14 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] hyundai: FCA11 shadow writer rotates at the byte cap instead
 of truncating the drive (0052)

Drive 15c lost its final 22 minutes to the FCA11 shadow writer's per-drive byte bound
(FCA11_SHADOW_MAX_BYTES = 25 MB): on reaching it the writer appended ONE k="trunc" record and
then stopped writing for the rest of that drive (self.truncated gates write()); 15e and 15f hit
the same cap (~8 k cap-hit frames each). Long drives are exactly the ones a brake tune needs, so
truncation destroys the useful data.

Fix - ROTATE instead of truncate. When the next record would cross the cap the writer closes the
current file with one k="rot" record (t_wall, part, bytes), shifts the existing generation chain
exactly as the constructor's _rotate() does (the oldest is dropped, .4->.5 ... current->.1) and
keeps writing in a fresh fca11-shadow.jsonl whose FIRST record is the same drive header plus
cont=<part> (identical oldlaw_armed / consts / note, a fresh t_wall), so a long drive becomes a
SEQUENCE of parts instead of a truncated file. The part count is bounded by the new
FCA11_SHADOW_MAX_PARTS = FCA11_SHADOW_KEEP (5): reaching the cap ON the last allowed part falls
back to the deployed truncation behaviour (one k="trunc", writes gated again), so the worst case
is 5 x 25 MB of parts per drive rather than a silent early stop.

Not restructured: _rotate(), the buffer/flush policy, the windowed frame records (including the
pre-context ring across a hop), the epilogue and the V3-fault paths are untouched. The cap check
now tries _rotate_at_cap() before setting self.truncated, and the byte / n_records / buffer
bookkeeping moved verbatim into _emit() so the continuation header can be written without a cap
check. Below the cap the writer is unchanged: a recorded shadow drive replayed through the real
Fca11LongBrake produces 453 records identical to the deployed module in every field except the
wall-clock ones (t_wall / t_wall_start / t_wall_end).

RETENTION: the generation chain is FCA11_SHADOW_KEEP = 5 TOTAL and a rotating drive reuses it, so
MAX_PARTS is set EQUAL to KEEP. A drive that rotates keeps every part of its OWN drive (start
included) and evicts the OLDER drives instead - the intended trade for validation, bounded at
5 x 25 MB = 125 MB per drive.

Tests (tests/test_fca11_shadow.py, new class TestShadowRotateAtCap, +5): a tiny cap (2000 B) with
setUp/tearDown save-restore of FCA11_SHADOW_MAX_BYTES / FCA11_SHADOW_MAX_PARTS - (a) writing
continues past the cap and the last record written is on disk (never k="trunc"); (b) every
rotated part exists, opens with a k="hdr" and ends with exactly one k="rot" carrying its own part
number and size; (c) the live part opens with k="hdr" cont=2 and otherwise the same header as
part 1; (d) the union of all parts == exactly the records written, each once; (e) at
FCA11_SHADOW_MAX_PARTS the last part ends with k="trunc" and write() is gated again. The pre-0052
byte-bound test is re-pinned to that fallback (MAX_PARTS = 1), which keeps it pinning the
deployed behaviour. 6/6 targeted mutations of the new code are killed; the shadow file alone passes 108/108 (pytest). Python only - no safety/
change, no firmware, no param/capnp change.
